.TH ENGINE1999-BASIC 7 "October 2026" "Engine 1999 @VERSION@" "Engine1999 Manual"
.SH NAME
engine1999\-basic \- the BASIC language of Engine1999
.SH DESCRIPTION
The BASIC of
.BR engine1999 (1)
and
.BR engine1999c (1)
is a classic line\-numbered BASIC with the structure of a modern one.
Programs are tokenized, compiled to the instructions of a stack machine and run by it.
This page is a summary; the complete reference is
.I basic\-language.md
in /usr/local/share/doc/engine1999/ (docs/ in the source tree), and the whole syntax is on one page in
.IR grammar.html .
.SH PROGRAMS
.TP
.B Lines
A line with a number from 0 to 999999 is stored in the program; a line without one runs at once (direct mode).
Several statements on a line are separated by a colon.
A comment starts with an apostrophe.
Words are not case sensitive; strings keep their case.
.TP
.B Targets
GOTO, GOSUB and the other jumps take a line number or a label
.RB ( LABEL
.IR name ).
.TP
.B Files
.B SAVE "name"
writes the binary form (\fIname.e99\fR),
.B SAVE "name",A
a text listing (\fIname.bas\fR);
.B LOAD "name"
finds either, and
.B MERGE
and
.B CHAIN
combine programs.
A text file may start with a
.B #!
line.
.TP
.B Ending
.B BYE [code]
or
.B QUIT [code]
ends the program and the Engine (or the built executable) with the exit status
.I code
(0 to 255).
.B EXIT
is the loop word, not a way to quit.
.SH DATA
Numbers are floating point; an integer variable has a
.B %
suffix; a string variable a
.B $
suffix (strings are binary safe, up to 65535 bytes).
Arrays have up to six dimensions
.RB ( DIM ).
.B LET
is optional.
Operators:
.B + \- * / \e MOD **
(\fB\e\fR and
.B MOD
are integer division and remainder),
the comparisons
.BR "= <> < > <= >=" ,
.BR "AND OR XOR NOT NAND NOR XNOR IMP NIMP EQV NEQV" ,
the shifts
.BR "SHL SHR ASL ASR LSHIFT RSHIFT" ,
and
.B +
for strings.
.SH STATEMENTS
.TP
.B Output and input
PRINT, INPUT, LINE INPUT, GET, WRITE, DATA, READ, RESTORE, CLS, LOCATE, COLOR
.TP
.B Control flow
IF ... THEN ... ELSE, UNLESS, FOR ... NEXT (also DOWNTO, FOR EACH), WHILE ... WEND, REPEAT ... UNTIL, UNTIL ... UEND,
EXIT, LEAVE, ITERATE, AGAIN, GOTO, GOSUB, RETURN, ON ... GOTO/GOSUB, POP, END
.TP
.B Procedures and functions
PROC ... END PROC and CALL, FUNC ... END FUNC, DEF FN, LOCAL, RETURN value, EXIT PROC
.TP
.B Errors
ON ERROR GOTO/GOSUB/OFF (or GOTO 0), RESUME, ERROR n, and the functions ERR$, ERR and ERL
.TP
.B Break key
ESC, Ctrl+C and (window) PAUSE/BREAK stop a running program; BREAK OFF makes ESC an ordinary key (INKEY$, GET) for the program, BREAK ON gives it its meaning back. Ctrl+C always stops, also in executables made with BUILD.
.TP
.B Keys held down
KEY("name") is \-1 while the key is held down and 0 otherwise ("A", "SPACE", "LEFT", "F1", "SHIFT", "ANY", ...), for any number of keys at once; unlike INKEY$ it knows when a key is let go. It works in the window only: the console host (and console executables) give the error "KEY is not available in this host", because a terminal never reports that a key is let go.
.TP
.B Game controllers
JOY(name$ [, player]) reads the game controllers (up to four, with one standard layout whatever the brand: buttons "A" "B" "X" "Y" "LB" "RB" "BACK" "START" "GUIDE" "LS" "RS" "UP" "DOWN" "LEFT" "RIGHT" give -1 or 0, the axes "LX" "LY" "RX" "RY" -1..1 with up negative and "LT" "RT" 0..1; also "CONNECTED", "COUNT", "BUTTONS", "PRESSED", "RELEASED", "EVENTS", "DEADZONE"); a player without a pad reads 0. JOY DEADZONE d sets the dead zone of the sticks (0..0.9). ON JOY GOSUB/GOTO/OFF runs a handler when a button is pressed or released or a pad is plugged in or out. The console host has no controllers (JOY("COUNT") is 0).
.TP
.B The mouse
MOUSE SHOW, HIDE, POS x,y, SHAPE name and TIMEOUT seconds control the mouse pointer, which is shown for 3 seconds after the mouse moves, a button is pressed or one of SHOW, POS, SHAPE is executed and then hidden so that the text can be read (TIMEOUT 0: it stays until HIDE). The shapes are ARROW, WAIT, BUSY, TEXT, HAND, GRAB, CROSS, SIZEH, SIZEV, MOVE, NO and HELP. The functions MOUSE("X") and MOUSE("Y") (0..639, 0..299), MOUSE("B") (buttons held: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle), MOUSE("PRESSED") and MOUSE("RELEASED") (buttons since the last call), MOUSE("WHEEL"), MOUSE("SHAPE") and MOUSE("VISIBLE") read it. ON MOUSE GOSUB/GOTO/OFF runs a handler when a button is pressed or released or the wheel turns (not on motion; highest priority of the event handlers). Works in the window and in the console host's screen mode (a terminal that reports the mouse: the pointer is the cell under it with swapped colors and its shape is the terminal's pointer, OSC 22; a cell is 8 x 12 pixels); elsewhere the console host gives an error.
.TP
.B Timers
AFTER, EVERY, WAIT, and the function REMAIN
.TP
.B Files and devices
OPEN, CLOSE, PRINT #, INPUT #, LINE INPUT #, GET #, SEEK, FIELD, LSET, RSET, GETREC, PUTREC, KILL, RENAME, MKDIR, CHDIR, ADDRESS, FILES
.TP
.B Sound
SOUND, PLAY, SFX, WAVE, TEMPO, PITCH, VOLUME, ENVELOPE, VIBRATO, TREMOLO
.TP
.B Graphics
GFX, TXT, TILE, SPRITE and DESIGN, with their own sub\-words (layers, palettes, drawing, tiles, sprites with collisions, text areas), and ON COLLISION. A program has the sprites 0 to 247; 248 to 255 belong to the engine (the mouse pointer is 255) and give "Illegal argument".
.TP
.B Program commands
LIST, LOAD, SAVE, RUN, NEW, CLEAR, DELETE, RENUM, MERGE, CHAIN, AUTO, EDIT, MONITOR, STOP, CONT, TRACE, BUILD, BYE, QUIT
.TP
.B Other
DIM, ERASE, RANDOMIZE, DEG, RAD, GRAD, POKE, SYMBOL
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.B Numbers
INT, FIX, FLOOR, CEIL, ABS, SGN, SQR, SIN, COS, TAN, ATN, EXP, LOG, LOG2, LOG10, RND, PI, VAL, ASC, LEN, CVI, CVL, CVS, CVD, PEEK, TI, REMAIN, PLAYING,
EOF, LOF, LOC, FRE, ARGC, RGB, POINT, ERL, ERR, KEY, MIN, MAX, ATAN2
.TP
.B Strings
CHR$, STR$, LEFT$, RIGHT$, MID$, BIN$, QUA$, OCT$, DEC$, HEX$, INPUT$, INKEY$, MKI$, MKL$, MKS$, MKD$, TIME$, DATE$, DATETIME$, PEEK$, ARG$, ENV$, ERR$, ADDRESS$, SPACE$, STRING$
.SH PROGRAM ARGUMENTS AND THE ENVIRONMENT
.B ARGC
is the number of program arguments,
.B ARG$(n)
the n\-th (0 is the program), and
.B ENV$(name$)
an environment variable.
Arguments come from the command line of
.BR engine1999c (1)
and of built executables.
.SH FILES AND DEVICES
A file name may start with a driver and a colon.
.TP
.B file:
Disk files (the default).
.TP
.B mem:
A RAM disk.
.TP
.B null:
Swallows output, reads nothing.
.TP
.B sh:
A shell command as a file:
.BR "OPEN ""sh:ls -l"" FOR INPUT AS #1" .
.TP
.B res:
Read\-only game assets in the directory
.IR res/ .
.TP
.B con:
The screen and keyboard as a channel.
.TP
.B play:
Lines written are music strings.
.TP
.B tcp:
A network connection (client).
.TP
.BR stdin: ", " stdout: ", " stderr:
The standard streams of the process.
.SH BUILDING EXECUTABLES
.B BUILD "name" [, WINDOW | CONSOLE]
makes a standalone executable from the program in memory, using only the system linker
.BR ld (1).
A
.B WINDOW
executable has the runtime of
.BR engine1999 (1)
and needs libSDL2 to run; a
.B CONSOLE
executable has the runtime of
.BR engine1999c (1)
and needs only the C and the math library.
Without the option the kind of the host is made.
Names that end in
.I .bas
or
.I .e99
are refused.
.PP
.B BUILD
needs the runtime kit, the files \fIengine1999.o\fR, \fIengine1999c.o\fR and \fIengine.lib\fR.
It looks for them, in this order, in the directory of the running program,
.IR ../lib/engine1999
beside it,
.BR $ENGINE1999_HOME ,
the current directory,
.IR /usr/local/lib/engine1999 ,
.I /usr/lib/engine1999
and
.IR /usr/local/share/engine1999 ;
the linker, the startup files and the libraries it finds by itself.
.B make install
puts the kit into
.IR /usr/local/lib/engine1999 .
.PP
The executable starts the program at once.
The command line is the program's (\fBARGC\fR, \fBARG$\fR), except
.B \-\-engine1999\-about
as the first argument, which prints the license notice of the runtime.
A console executable also takes
.BR \-\-engine1999\-screen ,
.B \-\-engine1999\-line
and
.B \-\-engine1999\-no\-dump
(first, before the program's words; see
.BR engine1999c (1)).
.PP
The runtime in an executable is covered by the Engine1999 Runtime Exception: the executable may be distributed under terms of the author's
choice if the runtime in it is the unmodified one and its notice stays.
See LICENSE\-EXCEPTION.
